    <node><name>1</name><description>No cos</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>nocos</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-2-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>That is the distance for \({@dl@}^\circ\) along a great circle. A parallel is a small circle, radius \(R\cos\varphi\), so multiply by \(\cos {@phi@}^\circ\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>2</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-2-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>2</name><description>Used sin</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>snm</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Use \(\cos {@phi@}^\circ\), not \(\sin {@phi@}^\circ\): the radius of the parallel is \(R\cos\varphi\).</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>3</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
    <node><name>3</name><description>Divided by cos</description><answertest>NumAbsolute</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>dvc</tans><testoptions>1</testoptions><quiet>0</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-4-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>You divided by \(\cos {@phi@}^\circ\). The parallel is shorter than the equator, so multiply.</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-4-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>On the parallel \({@phi@}^\circ\), a distance of \({@ans1@}\) nm spans \({@dispdp(yl,1)@}^\circ\) of longitude. Compare that with the difference in longitude between the two meridians. A parallel is a small circle, so each degree of longitude along it is shorter than \(60\) nm.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
